# This repository pins its own content by sha256: skills.lock records a digest
# per skill directory, computed over the WORKING TREE's bytes by
# scripts/generate_skills_lock.py, and .claude/hooks/skills-bootstrap.sh
# recomputes the same digest over a fresh clone before it installs anything.
# schemas/ is pinned the same way, by SCHEMA_SHA256 in check_agent_plugins.py.
#
# That only works if a checkout's bytes are the same on every platform. They
# are not by default: with `core.autocrlf=true` -- the Windows default, and set
# on ZENDA -- git rewrites LF to CRLF on the way out, so every digest computed
# on Windows disagreed with the one committed from Linux. The vendored schema
# reported "sha256 mismatch ... the vendored schema was modified" on every
# Windows clone, which is the worst possible failure mode for an integrity
# guard: it accuses you of tampering when nothing but the line endings changed.
#
# `-text` turns EOL conversion off entirely, in both directions, so the working
# tree is byte-for-byte what the blob holds. Not `text=auto eol=lf`: that
# normalises on the way IN as well, so a stale CRLF working tree keeps hashing
# clean and the digests stay silently wrong. With `-text` git reports those
# files as modified until they are re-checked-out, which is the loud version of
# the same news.
#
# Every tracked file here is text (89 of 89 at the time of writing), so there
# is nothing this can mangle.
#
# An existing Windows clone made before this file needs its working tree
# refreshed once, so the CRLF copies are replaced by the blobs' own bytes.
# Commit or stash your work first, then:
#
#     git ls-files -z | xargs -0 rm -f && git checkout -- .
#
# Two more obvious commands do NOT work, both measured on ZENDA:
#
#   * `git reset --hard` (and `git checkout-index -a -f`) leave the CRLF files
#     exactly as they are. Git decides a file is up to date from the index's
#     cached STAT data, and nothing about adding this file changes any working
#     file's size or mtime — so it never re-reads them and never rewrites them.
#     Deleting them first is what removes that shortcut.
#   * `git add --renormalize .` is worse than useless here: under `-text` it
#     reads the working tree WITHOUT converting and writes the CRLF bytes back
#     as new blobs, committing the problem instead of clearing it.
#
# The same stat cache is why `git status` reports a stale clone CLEAN while
# every file in it is still CRLF. A clone in that state passes `git status` and
# fails the digest checks, which is the confusing pair this note exists for; it
# only starts reporting the files as modified once something else touches them.
* -text
